prim:Int64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64" ; prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Integer ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ; prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:uefiName "INT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Int64 physical encoding: 64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Integer — the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). It is signed (straddling zero). UEFI exact-width naming precedent: INT64. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Int128 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT128" ; prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Integer ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ; prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Int128 physical encoding: 128 bits / 16 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Integer — the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). It is signed (straddling zero). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

# ---- Character encodings CHAR8/16 → Codepoint ---------------------------------------
prim:Char8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "CHAR8 (UEFI 8-bit character)" ; prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    prim:uefiName "CHAR8" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Char8 physical encoding: 8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). UEFI exact-width naming precedent: CHAR8. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Char16 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "CHAR16 (UEFI 16-bit UCS-2 character)" ; prim:bitWidth 16 ; prim:byteWidth 2 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    prim:uefiName "CHAR16" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Char16 physical encoding: 16 bits / 2 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). UEFI exact-width naming precedent: CHAR16. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

# ---- IEEE-754 floats Float16/32/64/80/128 → Real ------------------------------------
prim:Float16 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float16 (IEEE-754 half)" ; prim:bitWidth 16 ; prim:byteWidth 2 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Real ; prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 5 ; prim:mantissaBits 10 ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Float16 physical encoding: 16 bits / 2 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Real — the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). Its bits partition into the IEEE-754 sign, 5-bit biased exponent (dynamic range) and 10-bit significand (precision) fields. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Float32 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float32 (IEEE-754 single)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Real ; prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 8 ; prim:mantissaBits 23 ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Float32 physical encoding: 32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Real — the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). Its bits partition into the IEEE-754 sign, 8-bit biased exponent (dynamic range) and 23-bit significand (precision) fields. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Float64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float64 (IEEE-754 double)" ; prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Real ; prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ; prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Float64 physical encoding: 64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Real — the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). Its bits partition into the IEEE-754 sign, 11-bit biased exponent (dynamic range) and 52-bit significand (precision) fields. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Float80 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float80 (x87 double-extended)" ; prim:bitWidth 80 ; prim:byteWidth 10 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Real ; prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 15 ; prim:mantissaBits 64 ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Float80 physical encoding: 80 bits / 10 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Real — the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). Its bits partition into the IEEE-754 sign, 15-bit biased exponent (dynamic range) and 64-bit significand (precision) fields. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Float128 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float128 (IEEE-754 quad)" ; prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Real ; prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 15 ; prim:mantissaBits 112 ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Float128 physical encoding: 128 bits / 16 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Real — the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). Its bits partition into the IEEE-754 sign, 15-bit biased exponent (dynamic range) and 112-bit significand (precision) fields. As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .


# =====================================================================================
# Text encodings (Codepoint → byte). Variable-width encodings record their MAXIMUM
# occurrence width in bitWidth/byteWidth; the variability is a realization facet.
# =====================================================================================

prim:Ascii a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ASCII (7-bit code stored in one octet)" ; prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The Ascii physical encoding: 8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Latin1 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Latin-1 / ISO-8859-1 (single-octet code)" ; prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The Latin1 physical encoding: 8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Utf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 (variable 1..4 octets; max width recorded)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The Utf8 physical encoding: 32 bits / 4 bytes, endian-neutral. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:Utf16 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-16 (variable 2 or 4 octets; max width recorded)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The Utf16 physical encoding: 32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

# ---- UTF-16 LE / BE split (spec §9g): the byte-order variants of UTF-16, split out as
#      distinct encodings because for a multi-octet code unit the endianness is load-bearing
#      (the same code unit serializes to opposite octet order), so a single LE-defaulted
#      prim:Utf16 hid a real distinction. Both interpretAs prim:Codepoint (already punned).
prim:Utf16LE a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-16LE (little-endian code units)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The little-endian byte-order variant of UTF-16: each 16-bit code unit (and the two units of a surrogate pair) serialize least-significant octet first. Split from the neutral prim:Utf16 per spec §9g because endianness is a genuine coordinate for multi-octet code units — a byte stream is not decodable as UTF-16 without knowing LE from BE, and a byte-order-mark or an out-of-band declaration selects between this and prim:Utf16BE. interpretAs prim:Codepoint; its realization carries the same surrogate/normalization prim:EncodingLoss as the neutral form." .

prim:Utf16BE a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-16BE (big-endian code units)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:BE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The big-endian byte-order variant of UTF-16: each 16-bit code unit (and the two units of a surrogate pair) serialize most-significant octet first — the network/wire order and the form a byte-order-mark-less UTF-16 stream defaults to under the Unicode standard. Split from the neutral prim:Utf16 per spec §9g so the LE/BE distinction is a first-class carrier fact, not a hidden default. interpretAs prim:Codepoint; its realization carries the same surrogate/normalization prim:EncodingLoss as the neutral form." .

prim:Utf32 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-32 (fixed 4 octets)" ; prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The Utf32 physical encoding: 32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian. It interpretAs Codepoint — a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower — the mutual-colimit back-pointer that closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto its own formal type (spec §9d). As a concrete bit-layout it fixes how a span of bits over the carrier is read as a value, and it byte-descends through the physical carrier ladder to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .


# =====================================================================================
# RGB colour code space — three octet channels, ordinal 0..255 (JEPA colour substrate)
# =====================================================================================

prim:ColourSpace a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "ColourSpace" ;
    rdfs:comment "A colour code space — an organization of octet-valued channels that together denote a colour, the JEPA colour substrate of the unary law. Its canonical member here is prim:RGB, whose three channels Red, Green, and Blue are each exactly one prim:Octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal range 0..255. A ColourSpace relates its channels by prim:hasChannel; the channels' octet identity is what grounds colour into the physical byte carrier and, downstream, into the atom colour coordinates that ride the byte substrate." .

prim:RGB a prim:ColourSpace ;
    rdfs:label "RGB (Red/Green/Blue octet channels)" ;
    prim:hasChannel prim:Red , prim:Green , prim:Blue .

prim:Red a prim:Octet ;
    rdfs:label "Red channel (one Octet, ordinal 0..255)" ;
    prim:ordinalMin 0 ; prim:ordinalMax 255 .

prim:Green a prim:Octet ;
    rdfs:label "Green channel (one Octet, ordinal 0..255)" ;
    prim:ordinalMin 0 ; prim:ordinalMax 255 .

prim:Blue a prim:Octet ;
    rdfs:label "Blue channel (one Octet, ordinal 0..255)" ;
    prim:ordinalMin 0 ; prim:ordinalMax 255 .


# =====================================================================================
# R1 remediation — full-coverage physical carriers (spec §9a/§9b/§9c/§9g, plan Task R1).
# =====================================================================================
# The panel NO-GO recorded that 50 of 59 tower classes were ungrounded because the physical
# olog exposed no carrier for the composite, variable-length, opaque, or temporal formal
# types. This block adds a dedicated prim:PhysicalEncoding for every concrete tower type
# realization.ttl now grounds, each carrying the mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs
# to its OWN formal type (so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is
# reflexive per spec §9d) with prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (scalar cap) or prim:byteCount ≤ 128
# (ByteVector cap, spec §9c). The former ancestry cases (URI/IRI/QName taxonomically under URN;
# Latitude/Longitude under Coordinate; Ordinal/Categorical under Enumeration) are now each grounded
# DIRECTLY: every one carries its OWN prim:realizesFrom arrow, its OWN reflexive prim:interpretAs
# carrier and its OWN prim:Primitive (authored in realization.ttl / taiji.ttl). The supertype link
# is taxonomic subtyping only, NOT the grounding mechanism (spec §9a). Corpus-agnostic: pure shape.
#
# Two width caps (spec §9c): a fixed-width SCALAR/PRODUCT encoding declares prim:bitWidth
# (≤ 128, the widest being the 128-bit Complex/Interval/Measurement products of two 64-bit
# components). A variable-length BYTEVECTOR-family or serialized encoding is octet-granular:
# prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet) and prim:byteCount records
# the occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128). The 0-byte prim:VoidEmpty is the honest edge —
# bitWidth 0, byteCount 0 — the uninhabited empty product.


# ---- Punning witnesses (OWL 2): every FormalType a dedicated encoding below interprets
#      back to is asserted as an INDIVIDUAL a prim:FormalType, so the non-inference round-trip
#      ASK q_colimit_roundtrip (?e interpretAs ?f . ?f a prim:FormalType) closes for each new
#      encoding. The classes, tower arrows and ≥200-char comments are authored in formal.ttl;
#      these are additive type facts (as physical.ttl already does for Bool/Natural/Integer/
#      Real/Codepoint). Every punned type here carries its own prim:realizesAs in realization.ttl.
prim:Rational      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Complex       a prim:FormalType .
prim:Imaginary     a prim:FormalType .
prim:Bignum        a prim:FormalType .
prim:Decimal       a prim:FormalType .
prim:String        a prim:FormalType .
prim:Grapheme      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Date          a prim:FormalType .
prim:Time          a prim:FormalType .
prim:DateTime      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Duration      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Interval      a prim:FormalType .
prim:URN           a prim:FormalType .
prim:BlankNode     a prim:FormalType .
prim:Literal       a prim:FormalType .
prim:Triple        a prim:FormalType .
prim:Quad          a prim:FormalType .
prim:RDFGraph      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Tuple         a prim:FormalType .
prim:List          a prim:FormalType .
prim:Set           a prim:FormalType .
prim:Bag           a prim:FormalType .
prim:Map           a prim:FormalType .
prim:Vector        a prim:FormalType .
prim:Tensor        a prim:FormalType .
prim:Sum           a prim:FormalType .
prim:Optional      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Frame         a prim:FormalType .
prim:Void          a prim:FormalType .
prim:Quantity      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Dimension     a prim:FormalType .
prim:UnitOfMeasure a prim:FormalType .
prim:Currency      a prim:FormalType .
prim:Measurement   a prim:FormalType .
prim:Coordinate    a prim:FormalType .
prim:Point         a prim:FormalType .
prim:Line          a prim:FormalType .
prim:Polygon       a prim:FormalType .
prim:CRS           a prim:FormalType .
prim:Enumeration   a prim:FormalType .
prim:Kleene3       a prim:FormalType .
prim:Blob          a prim:FormalType .
prim:Hash          a prim:FormalType .


# ---- Number tower composites -------------------------------------------------------
prim:ProductRationalIntInt a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator) → Rational" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 4 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Rational ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical rational carrier: a categorical product of two two's-complement INT32 components — numerator then non-zero denominator — laid contiguously little-endian for a total bitWidth 64, aligned to the 4-byte component boundary with no padding. Unlike a lossy float realization of ℚ this pair holds the ratio exactly in value; the typed cost is that each component is width-bounded, so a numerator or denominator outside the INT32 window overflows (prim:OverflowDomain). interpretAs prim:Rational closes the mutual colimit reflexively." .

prim:ProductComplexFloat64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary) → Complex" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Complex ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical complex carrier: a product of two IEEE-754 Float64 components — real part then imaginary part — contiguous little-endian for the maximum scalar bitWidth 128 (the two-64-bit-component ceiling of the scalar cap), 8-byte aligned with no padding. Each component carries the finite-grid IEEE-754 rounding of a Real, so the product's realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754. interpretAs prim:Complex makes the round-trip reflexive; Complex is the join of the real and imaginary axes this pair encodes." .

prim:ImaginaryFloat64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float64 (imaginary coordinate) → Imaginary" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Imaginary ;
    rdfs:comment "The imaginary-number carrier: a single IEEE-754 Float64 holding the real multiple of the imaginary unit i (the coefficient of a zero-real-part complex), 8 bytes little-endian. It is structurally a Float64 but interprets back to prim:Imaginary rather than prim:Real, witnessing that the top of the number tower is the join of a real axis and an imaginary axis; its realization carries the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect of any IEEE-754 real." .

prim:ByteVectorBignum a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(≤128 bytes, two's-complement magnitude) → Bignum" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:BE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Bignum ;
    rdfs:comment "The arbitrary-precision-integer carrier that replaces the removed UINT128 isProvisional dodge (spec §9b): an ordered prim:ByteVector of up to 128 byte occurrences holding a two's-complement magnitude big-endian. It is octet-granular — prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence granule, prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of the second width cap (spec §9c, up to 1024 bits). A mathematically unbounded Bignum meets this bounded carrier, so the realization Frame carries prim:MayOverflow. interpretAs prim:Bignum closes the colimit reflexively — the round-trip lands back on Bignum, not on Natural as the old Uint128 proxy did." .


# ---- Text tower --------------------------------------------------------------------
prim:StringUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream → String" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:String ;
    rdfs:comment "The string carrier: the ordered UTF-8 octet stream that a finite sequence of Codepoints serializes into, octet-granular (bitWidth 8 per occurrence, byteCount the vector ceiling) and endian-neutral because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering. It interprets back to prim:String (the whole sequence) rather than to a single prim:Codepoint, so the round-trip is reflexive at the String level; its realization carries prim:EncodingLoss for surrogate/normalization edge cases inherited from the codepoint encoding." .

prim:GraphemeUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream (one cluster) → Grapheme" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Grapheme ;
    rdfs:comment "The grapheme-cluster carrier: the UTF-8 octet run of one user-perceived character — a base codepoint plus any combining marks — octet-granular and endian-neutral. It interprets back to prim:Grapheme rather than prim:Codepoint precisely because a cluster is a sequence of codepoints, not one; the reflexive round-trip therefore lands at the human-visible unit. Its realization carries prim:EncodingLoss (normalization-form differences change the cluster's byte run)." .


# ---- Temporal tower ----------------------------------------------------------------
prim:DateInt32Days a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT32 (days since epoch) → Date" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Date ;
    rdfs:comment "The calendar-date carrier: a signed two's-complement INT32 count of whole days from a fixed epoch (proleptic Gregorian 1970-01-01), little-endian, 32 bits. A day count rather than an instant tick because a Date is a timezone-free civil coordinate naming a whole day; the signed 32-bit window spans roughly ±5.8 million years so the typed cost is the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain. interpretAs prim:Date closes the colimit reflexively at the Date level, not at prim:Integer as a bare INT32 would." .

prim:TimeInt64Nanos a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 (nanoseconds since midnight) → Time" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Time ;
    rdfs:comment "The time-of-day carrier: a signed INT64 count of nanoseconds since midnight within an unspecified day, little-endian, 64 bits — enough to name any instant of the daily cycle to nanosecond resolution with room for leap-second overrun. A Time alone names no absolute instant (it needs a Date and zone), so this interprets back to prim:Time, not prim:Instant; the width-bounded tick count carries prim:OverflowDomain." .

prim:DateTimeInt64Tz a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT64 epoch, INT16 tz-offset-minutes) → DateTime" ;
    prim:bitWidth 80 ; prim:byteWidth 10 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:DateTime ;
    rdfs:comment "The civil date-and-time carrier: a product of a signed INT64 epoch-tick component and a signed INT16 timezone-offset-in-minutes component, little-endian, total bitWidth 80. The offset component is the load-bearing timezone facet that pins the civil naming to an absolute Instant; its presence is what distinguishes a zoned DateTime from a floating local one. interpretAs prim:DateTime reflexively; the epoch tick's finite window carries prim:OverflowDomain." .

prim:DurationInt64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 (signed tick count) → Duration" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Duration ;
    rdfs:comment "The duration carrier: a signed INT64 count of a fixed tick unit (nanoseconds), little-endian, 64 bits, denoting an unanchored length of time with no position on the time line — the difference of two Instants. Distinct from prim:Interval (anchored, a start-plus-end product) and prim:Instant (a point); it interprets back to prim:Duration. The finite tick window and any conversion between tick granularities carry the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect." .

prim:ProductIntervalInstant a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT64 start-epoch, INT64 end-epoch) → Interval" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Interval ;
    rdfs:comment "The anchored-span carrier: a product of two signed INT64 epoch-tick Instant components — start then end — little-endian, total bitWidth 128, the two-64-bit-component ceiling of the scalar cap. Unlike an unanchored prim:Duration this pair pins both endpoints on the time line; the half-open [start,end) inclusivity is a facet recorded on the realization rather than assumed. interpretAs prim:Interval reflexively; each epoch component's finite window carries prim:OverflowDomain." .


# ---- Identifier tower: URN root here; URI/IRI/QName each grounded DIRECTLY (own carrier) --------
prim:UrnUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream (interpreted name) → URN" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:URN ;
    rdfs:comment "The Uniform-Resource-Name carrier and, transitively, the whole identifier tower's grounding: an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream over a byte carrier, octet-granular and endian-neutral. URN is the load-bearing root of the identifier tower (S/O/P are URN towers); URI, IRI and QName are taxonomic subtypes of it but are each grounded DIRECTLY by their own prim:realizesFrom arrow and their own reflexive carrier (UriUtf8, IriUtf8, QNameUtf8 in realization.ttl), not through this URN carrier (spec §9a). interpretAs prim:URN closes the colimit reflexively; percent-encoding/normalization of non-ASCII octets is the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries." .

prim:BlankNodeLabel a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 graph-local label → BlankNode" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:BlankNode ;
    rdfs:comment "The blank-node carrier: an interpreted UTF-8 graph-local label (for example _:b0), octet-granular and endian-neutral. Modelled separately from prim:UrnUtf8 because a BlankNode is precisely the identifier that has no Uniform Resource Name — it is not under URN in the formal tower — so it needs its own carrier. Its scope is a single graph/document and the label must be freshened on merge; that graph-local re-labelling is the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries. interpretAs prim:BlankNode reflexively." .


# ---- RDF-term tower ----------------------------------------------------------------
prim:LiteralUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 lexical form + datatype/lang coordinate → Literal" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Literal ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-literal carrier: the UTF-8 octet stream of a lexical form paired with a datatype-IRI or language-tag coordinate, octet-granular and endian-neutral. A typed literal is dual-natured — an RDF term whose datatype grounds it into the Number/Text/Temporal towers via the W3C datatype algebra — and this carrier holds the lexical bytes from which that value is parsed. interpretAs prim:Literal reflexively; lexical-to-value canonicalization (e.g. '1.0' vs '1.00') is the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries." .

prim:TripleNTriples a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "N-Triples UTF-8 line → Triple" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Triple ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-triple carrier: one N-Triples UTF-8 line serializing an ordered subject-predicate-object statement — a product of three independently typed URN/term coordinates, not a flat row. Octet-granular and endian-neutral. interpretAs prim:Triple reflexively; the N-Triples serialization's whitespace/escaping variance and its round-trip fidelity against the abstract triple are the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries." .

prim:QuadNQuads a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "N-Quads UTF-8 line → Quad" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Quad ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-quad carrier: one N-Quads UTF-8 line serializing a triple extended with a fourth graph-name coordinate — a wider product than a Triple, not a subtype of it. Octet-granular and endian-neutral. interpretAs prim:Quad reflexively; the graph coordinate is itself an identifier-tower value, and the serialization's escaping/whitespace variance is the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries." .

prim:RDFGraphTurtle a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Turtle UTF-8 document → RDFGraph" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:RDFGraph ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-graph carrier: a Turtle UTF-8 document serializing a set of Triples — the composite top of the RDF-term tower and the very shape this whole ontology is expressed in. Octet-granular and endian-neutral. interpretAs prim:RDFGraph reflexively; the choice of concrete syntax (Turtle vs N-Triples), prefix/blank-node relabelling and statement ordering mean the byte round-trip is not the identity on the abstract graph without a canonicalization discipline — the prim:EncodingLoss the realization carries." .


# ---- Aggregate tower ---------------------------------------------------------------
prim:TupleConcat a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ordered component concatenation → Tuple" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Tuple ;
    rdfs:comment "The tuple/finite-product carrier: the in-order concatenation of the byte encodings of a fixed-arity, heterogeneous component list, octet-granular. Its arity is fixed and part of its identity, so no length prefix is needed; alignment and padding between components are facets. This is the general shape behind the Rational and Complex pairs and the Frame product. interpretAs prim:Tuple reflexively; a faithful in-order concatenation is lossless framing, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:ListLenPrefixed a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "length-prefixed homogeneous run → List" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:List ;
    rdfs:comment "The list/sequence carrier: a length prefix followed by that many contiguous element encodings of one element type, octet-granular little-endian. Order is part of identity, so unlike Set and Bag it never reorders and duplicate elements are positionally significant. It is the parent shape of the fixed-length prim:Vector. interpretAs prim:List reflexively; a faithful length-prefixed run preserves order and multiplicity exactly, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:SetSortedLenPrefixed a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "canonically sorted length-prefixed distinct run → Set" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Set ;
    rdfs:comment "The set carrier: a length prefix followed by the distinct element encodings laid in a canonical sort order chosen only for byte-level determinism, not for identity — a Set is unordered and duplicate-free. Because the abstract Set has no order, imposing the canonical serialization order is a real, typed transformation: the realization carries prim:OrderingCanonicalization (a different input element order yields the same bytes, so the mapping is not the identity on presentation order). interpretAs prim:Set reflexively." .

prim:BagLenCounts a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "length-prefixed element+multiplicity run → Bag" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Bag ;
    rdfs:comment "The bag/multiset carrier: a length prefix followed by (element, multiplicity-count) pairs, octet-granular little-endian, where the per-element count is significant but position is not. A Bag is the parent of Set (a Set is a Bag whose every multiplicity is at most one). interpretAs prim:Bag reflexively; storing the counts explicitly preserves multiplicity exactly and any serialization order of the pairs is an arbitrary facet, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect on value (multiplicities are exact)." .

prim:MapLenKv a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "length-prefixed key-value pair run → Map" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Map ;
    rdfs:comment "The map/associative-array carrier: a length prefix followed by that many (key, value) encoding pairs with unique keys, octet-granular little-endian — a partial function from a key type to a value type. Any hashing or key-ordering is a realization facet, not identity. interpretAs prim:Map reflexively; a faithful key-value run preserves the finite function exactly (unique keys, paired values), so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:VectorFixedArray a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "dense fixed-length equal-width array → Vector" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Vector ;
    rdfs:comment "The vector carrier: a dense contiguous run of a fixed count of equal-width numeric element encodings with a stated component order and endianness, octet-granular so its byte layout is directly index-addressable. Vector is the rank-1 case of Tensor and the pinned-length subtype of List; it feeds the AOB tensor block. interpretAs prim:Vector reflexively; a dense equal-width array preserves the components and their order exactly, so the framing realization carries prim:NoEffect (the components' own encodings carry any rounding)." .

prim:TensorRowMajor a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "shape descriptor + row-major dense block → Tensor" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Tensor ;
    rdfs:comment "The tensor carrier and the AOB tensor block's physical home: a shape descriptor (a tuple of per-axis extents) plus a dense row-major block of the numeric components, octet-granular little-endian. The explicit shape-and-stride metadata is what distinguishes a rank-n Tensor from a flat List. interpretAs prim:Tensor reflexively; a faithful shape-plus-row-major block preserves rank, shape and every component in order, so the framing realization carries prim:NoEffect." .


# ---- Algebraic-constructor tower ---------------------------------------------------
prim:SumTagPayload a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8 tag + selected payload → Sum" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Sum ;
    rdfs:comment "The sum/coproduct/tagged-union carrier: a discriminating UINT8 tag selecting the active alternative followed by that alternative's payload encoding, octet-granular little-endian. Sum is the categorical dual of the Tuple product and the shape behind the byte-vector Empty|Link and the admission Accepted|Rejected states. interpretAs prim:Sum reflexively; a faithful tag-plus-payload names exactly one alternative and preserves its payload, so the framing realization carries prim:NoEffect (the tag width is a facet)." .

prim:OptionalPresence a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "presence tag (0|1) + optional payload → Optional" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Optional ;
    rdfs:comment "The optional/maybe carrier: a one-byte presence tag (0 = none, 1 = some) followed, when present, by the payload encoding — the two-alternative tagged union none|some(value), a subtype of Sum. This is how the floor represents nullability structurally without conflating it with Kleene3 unknown: absence is a structural fact, not a third truth value. interpretAs prim:Optional reflexively; a faithful presence-plus-payload distinguishes absent from present exactly, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:FrameProduct a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(output encoding, effect coordinate) → Frame" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Frame ;
    rdfs:comment "The Frame carrier — the physical home of the unary law's Yoneda point: a product of an output-coordinate encoding and exactly one error-or-effect coordinate, octet-granular. It is a Tuple whose two coordinates are fixed as output (X) and effect (Y), the T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) shape the realization monad is the Kleisli category of. interpretAs prim:Frame reflexively; a faithful two-coordinate product preserves both the output and its typed effect exactly, so the framing realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:VoidEmpty a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "empty 0-byte encoding (uninhabited) → Void" ;
    prim:bitWidth 0 ; prim:byteWidth 0 ; prim:byteCount 0 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Void ;
    rdfs:comment "The void/initial-type carrier: the empty 0-byte encoding — zero bits, zero bytes, zero occurrences — the physical nothing of the empty coproduct. It is the honest edge that keeps universality total without a dodge (spec §9f): Void has no values, so a value of Void can never be constructed, and this carrier's realization Frame carries the prim:Uninhabited effect (not prim:NoEffect — a lossless map of nothing is still the map of an impossible value). Distinct from the zero-width prim:UnitEncoding, which is inhabited by exactly one value; VoidEmpty is inhabited by none. interpretAs prim:Void reflexively." .


# ---- Quantity tower ----------------------------------------------------------------
prim:ProductQuantity a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit index) → Quantity" ;
    prim:bitWidth 80 ; prim:byteWidth 10 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Quantity ;
    rdfs:comment "The quantity carrier: a product of a Float64 magnitude component and a UINT16 unit-of-measure index component, little-endian, total bitWidth 80. A Quantity is a magnitude tagged with a unit (5 kilograms, 300 kelvin); unit conversions are typed arrows carrying a rescaling effect. It is the parent of Measurement (a Quantity plus an uncertainty). interpretAs prim:Quantity reflexively; the Float64 magnitude carries the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 the realization Frame reports." .

prim:DimensionExponents a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8 dimensional index → Dimension" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Dimension ;
    rdfs:comment "The physical-dimension carrier: a UINT8 index into the registry of base-dimension exponent vectors (length, mass, time and their products such as length·time⁻¹ for velocity), one octet. A Dimension classifies which units are mutually convertible — quantities add or compare only when their Dimensions match — and reduces dimensional analysis to integer-vector arithmetic on the indexed exponent formula. interpretAs prim:Dimension reflexively; a small-integer index is an exact, lossless label, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:UnitOfMeasureIndex a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT16 unit registry index → UnitOfMeasure" ;
    prim:bitWidth 16 ; prim:byteWidth 2 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    rdfs:comment "The unit-of-measure carrier: a UINT16 index into a registry of named reference magnitudes (metre, second, kilogram, and derived units such as newton), each entry carrying a conversion factor and offset to the SI base of its Dimension. Named UnitOfMeasure to avoid colliding with the type-theoretic terminal prim:Unit. It is the taxonomic parent of Currency, but Currency is grounded DIRECTLY by its own ISO-4217 carrier (CurrencyIso4217) and its own prim:realizesFrom arrow (spec §9a), not through this UnitOfMeasure carrier. interpretAs prim:UnitOfMeasure reflexively; an exact registry index is lossless, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:CurrencyIso4217 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "3×CHAR8 ISO-4217 alphabetic code → Currency" ;
    prim:bitWidth 24 ; prim:byteWidth 3 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Currency ;
    rdfs:comment "The currency carrier: three CHAR8 octets holding an ISO-4217 alphabetic code (USD, EUR, JPY), bitWidth 24, a subtype grounding of UnitOfMeasure for the dimension of monetary value. Unlike physical units a currency's conversion factors are time-varying exchange rates, a documented facet of any conversion realization; a monetary Quantity is a magnitude tagged with this code. interpretAs prim:Currency reflexively; the fixed three-letter code is an exact, lossless label, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

prim:ProductMeasurement a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 value, Float64 uncertainty) → Measurement" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ; prim:interpretAs prim:Measurement ;
    rdfs:comment "The measurement carrier: a product of two Float64 components — the value and its paired uncertainty (error bar / standard deviation) in the same unit — little-endian, total bitWidth 128. Measurement is a Quantity that additionally records how well it is known: a bare Quantity asserts an exact magnitude, a Measurement admits its own imprecision so error propagation is explicit. interpretAs prim:Measurement reflexively; both Float64 components carry the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 the realization Frame reports." .


# ---- Geospatial tower: Coordinate root here; Latitude/Longitude each grounded DIRECTLY ---------
prim:CoordinateFloat64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float64 (axis position) → Coordinate" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Coordinate ;
    rdfs:comment "The coordinate carrier: a single IEEE-754 Float64 scalar position along one axis of a coordinate reference system, 8 bytes little-endian. Latitude and Longitude are taxonomic subtypes of Coordinate but are each grounded DIRECTLY by their own prim:realizesFrom arrow and their own reflexive carrier (LatitudeFloat64, LongitudeFloat64), not through this Coordinate carrier (spec §9a). A Coordinate has meaning only relative to its CRS and axis order, which travel with it as facets. interpretAs prim:Coordinate reflexively; the Float64 axis value carries the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect." .

prim:PointWkb a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Well-Known-Binary point geometry → Point" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Point ;
    rdfs:comment "The point-geometry carrier: a Well-Known-Binary (WKB) encoding of a single location — an ordered tuple of Float64 coordinates (longitude, latitude, optional elevation) with a byte-order flag and geometry-type header, octet-granular. The axis order (lon-lat vs lat-lon) is a load-bearing facet recorded on the realization, not guessed. interpretAs prim:Point reflexively; because the embedded coordinates are IEEE-754 Float64 the realization carries prim:RoundingIEEE754." .

prim:LineWkb a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Well-Known-Binary linestring geometry → Line" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Line ;
    rdfs:comment "The line/linestring-geometry carrier: a WKB encoding of an ordered sequence of two or more Points describing a one-dimensional path, octet-granular; its identity includes vertex order and count, and all vertices share one CRS. interpretAs prim:Line reflexively; because the embedded vertex coordinates are IEEE-754 Float64 the realization carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the same axis-order facet as its points is recorded once for the whole geometry)." .

prim:PolygonWkb a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Well-Known-Binary polygon geometry → Polygon" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Polygon ;
    rdfs:comment "The polygon-geometry carrier: a WKB encoding of a two-dimensional area bounded by one closed exterior ring of Points and zero or more interior rings (holes), octet-granular. Ring closure (first vertex equals last) and winding order are part of well-formedness and are recorded so exterior and holes are unambiguous. interpretAs prim:Polygon reflexively; because the embedded ring coordinates are IEEE-754 Float64 the realization carries prim:RoundingIEEE754." .

prim:CrsEpsg a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 EPSG code → CRS" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:CRS ;
    rdfs:comment "The coordinate-reference-system carrier: a UINT32 EPSG code (for example 4326 for WGS-84) identifying the datum, coordinate system and axis order that give Coordinates and geometries their meaning, 32 bits little-endian. Every Point, Line and Polygon must reference a CRS; it seeds the S/O/P-tower CRS sub-project. interpretAs prim:CRS reflexively; a registry code is an exact, lossless identifier (the projection parameters it names are looked up, not lost), so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .


# ---- Enum tower: Enumeration root here; Ordinal/Categorical each grounded DIRECTLY -------------
prim:EnumerationIndex a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 member code → Enumeration" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Enumeration ;
    rdfs:comment "The enumeration carrier: a UINT32 code assigned to each member of a finite, explicitly named set, 32 bits little-endian. Ordinal and Categorical are taxonomic subtypes of Enumeration but are each grounded DIRECTLY by their own prim:realizesFrom arrow and their own reflexive carrier (Ordinal_Uint32Index, Categorical_Uint32Index), not through this Enumeration carrier (spec §9a). The code-to-member mapping is part of the enumeration's identity, not an arbitrary realization choice; Ordinal further requires order-preserving codes and Categorical treats codes as bare labels. interpretAs prim:Enumeration reflexively; an exact member code is a lossless label, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .


# ---- Boolean tower (Kleene3; Bool already realized via BOOLEAN) ---------------------
prim:Kleene3Uint8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8 (0=false, 1=true, 2=unknown) → Kleene3" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Kleene3 ;
    rdfs:comment "The three-valued-logic carrier: a UINT8 holding 0 (false), 1 (true) or 2 (unknown) — three states that do not fit one bit, which is why Kleene3 realizes in a byte rather than a Bit. Kleene3 is the supertype of Bool (Boolean logic is Kleene logic with the unknown value forbidden) and the formal home of the SQL NULL and structured/unstructured indeterminacy. interpretAs prim:Kleene3 reflexively; the three-value code is exact and lossless, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect." .


# ---- Binary/opaque tower -----------------------------------------------------------
prim:ByteVectorBlob a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(≤128 bytes, opaque) → Blob" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Blob ;
    rdfs:comment "The opaque-binary carrier: an ordered prim:ByteVector of up to 128 raw octets with no interpreted internal structure — the hexadecimal/serialized-binary payload a format leaves uninterpreted, the root of the binary/opaque tower. Octet-granular (bitWidth 8 per occurrence, byteCount the vector ceiling), endian-neutral because the bytes are uninterpreted. interpretAs prim:Blob reflexively; storing raw octets verbatim is exact, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect (any structure the bytes may have is deferred to a more specific type)." .

prim:ByteVectorHash a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(32 bytes fixed, sha256) → Hash" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 32 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Hash ;
    rdfs:comment "The digest carrier that grounds the SP3 z-coordinate: a fixed-length prim:ByteVector of exactly 32 opaque octets (the sha256 width), a subtype of Blob adding the fixed-width and producing-algorithm invariants. The atom z-coordinate is derived as the UINT16 of the first two octets of such a digest. Octet-granular, byteCount pinned to 32, endian-neutral. interpretAs prim:Hash reflexively; storing the digest octets verbatim is exact, so the realization carries prim:NoEffect (the algorithm identity is a recorded facet)." .
